-**Two channels, and what this does not predict.** $\Lambda_k$ rises through a *b-channel*, which exists only because later mentions are named less often, and a *$\bar G$-channel*, which exists under any missingness. Because the $k$-reference golds are nested, the $\bar G$-channel alone already orders the slopes in $k$, so the ordering is expected even if objects are missing from references completely at random (SCAR), and confirming it supports the pattern, not the propensity model. A test against a size-matched random-deletion null, which is the SCAR case, can separate the b-channel from the $\bar G$-channel; the registered X4 and X5 tests of Section 6.6 are such tests. The decomposition does not predict where the sign changes, the negative one-reference sign, or the sign with several references, which was predicted positive and failed on two English LVLM families and on XM3600; on PASCAL-50S neither sign-change location was predicted either (Section 6). A second axis is gold precision: a large union of references also holds classes that no label confirms (on PASCAL-50S, 641 image–class pairs are named in the references but absent from the VOC labels; Supplementary Table S7.6), either label omissions or reference errors, so such a union is not a gold of perfect precision; the decomposition assumes precise gold.<!-- rev4 (A_SPIC_2026-11-03 META_REVIEW item 11; 2026-11-04 writer, proposal for the PI rewrite): 641 = Supplementary S7 G0 paragraph and Table S7.6 'references only' total (g0.json, sha256 23e12cbd179e :: sum_refs50_not_voc; new manifest row); consensus-gold sensitivity is [EXP -> coordinator] --><!-- rev1 (item 14): data/results/p50/p50_decide.json (0da4cab90fe9) :: analysis_set.P50-NS.{refs50|coco80, refs10_randk|V20, refs20_randk|V20, refs50|V20} (manifest keyed) --><!-- rev2 (CC-181 W8): condensed; original paragraph moved verbatim to Supplementary S10 -->
